WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.212 --> 00:00:02.712
(regal music)

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:31.686
(processional drumming)

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:42.407
(processional music)

00:02:49.578 --> 00:02:50.828
- Good morning.

00:02:54.721 --> 00:02:57.388
To our newest class of scholars,

00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:01.258
welcome to Yale.

00:03:02.236 --> 00:03:05.790
I'm delighted to join
your dean, Dean Cooley,

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.722
and other members of the Yale community

00:03:08.722 --> 00:03:13.555
in marking the beginning of
your graduate studies at Yale.

00:03:15.806 --> 00:03:19.618
You know, I was in your
position back in 1981.

00:03:19.618 --> 00:03:24.249
I came to Yale as a graduate
student from Stanford.

00:03:24.249 --> 00:03:27.797
I had worked in the Bay Area for a year.

00:03:27.797 --> 00:03:29.954
And when I arrived here,

00:03:29.954 --> 00:03:31.506
I had a pretty clear understanding

00:03:31.506 --> 00:03:34.483
of the work I wanted to
do in graduate school.

00:03:34.483 --> 00:03:37.483
And I'm sure many of you do as well.

00:03:38.855 --> 00:03:43.772
But I really had no idea how
much Yale would shape my life,

00:03:45.015 --> 00:03:47.932
both professionally and personally.

00:03:49.096 --> 00:03:52.994
I also really couldn't have
predicted my career trajectory,

00:03:52.994 --> 00:03:55.882
but that's another story.

00:03:55.882 --> 00:03:58.467
As a graduate student,

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:00.312
I knew I wanted to be

00:04:00.312 --> 00:04:03.628
a clinically-trained social psychologist,

00:04:03.628 --> 00:04:05.591
to study human emotion,

00:04:05.591 --> 00:04:08.385
and, eventually, my goal was to have

00:04:08.385 --> 00:04:11.218
a research group and a laboratory.

00:04:13.043 --> 00:04:17.960
But I never would've thought
that the mentoring I received,

00:04:19.112 --> 00:04:21.315
the insights I gained,

00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:25.482
and the relationships I
developed would lead here.

00:04:28.217 --> 00:04:29.050
In graduate school,

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.879
I didn't foresee that what I'd learned

00:04:31.879 --> 00:04:34.212
would one day help me serve,

00:04:35.658 --> 00:04:38.325
serve this wonderful university,

00:04:39.366 --> 00:04:43.116
a beautiful, complex
community of individuals

00:04:44.447 --> 00:04:46.364
from all walks of life.

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:50.238
Ironically, although I came here

00:04:50.238 --> 00:04:51.911
to conduct research on emotion

00:04:51.911 --> 00:04:56.547
and that later led to work
on emotional intelligence,

00:04:56.547 --> 00:04:59.466
my mentors in the psychology department

00:04:59.466 --> 00:05:01.636
led me to study, as well,

00:05:01.636 --> 00:05:04.210
how people process
information about health

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:07.332
and how to design messages about health

00:05:07.332 --> 00:05:10.769
that motivate people
to protect their health

00:05:10.769 --> 00:05:12.982
and the health of others.

00:05:12.982 --> 00:05:16.493
Here we are, at the end
of a pandemic, I hope,

00:05:16.493 --> 00:05:19.660
and that experience has come in handy.

00:05:21.528 --> 00:05:23.064
So each of these lines of work

00:05:23.064 --> 00:05:26.982
seems so applicable at the present moment,

00:05:26.982 --> 00:05:30.554
but it's all more than
I really could imagine

00:05:30.554 --> 00:05:33.637
at the time I was in graduate school.

00:05:34.682 --> 00:05:36.564
Well, I don't have to tell you

00:05:36.564 --> 00:05:40.231
how unpredictable this
world of ours can be.

00:05:41.494 --> 00:05:46.494
The COVID-19 pandemic has
reshaped our lives and our society

00:05:46.583 --> 00:05:49.666
in ways that few of us could imagine.

00:05:51.412 --> 00:05:55.880
There are many changes we
made during the last two years

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:57.401
that we're going to keep in place.

00:05:57.401 --> 00:06:00.736
We call them COVID keepers, right?

00:06:00.736 --> 00:06:03.684
Place has permanently changed.

00:06:03.684 --> 00:06:06.351
COVID-19 has reshaped our lives.

00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:11.547
Alongside this historic
public health crisis,

00:06:11.547 --> 00:06:16.214
we've also witnessed a national
reckoning around racism.

00:06:19.202 --> 00:06:20.436
We've witnessed the perils

00:06:20.436 --> 00:06:23.353
of conflict and political upheaval.

00:06:25.322 --> 00:06:27.465
We've witnessed the continued impact

00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:30.132
of climate change on our planet.

00:06:32.494 --> 00:06:34.407
In the midst of this kind of turbulence,

00:06:34.407 --> 00:06:37.424
we know that the world needs solutions

00:06:37.424 --> 00:06:40.893
from the kinds of research and scholarship

00:06:40.893 --> 00:06:43.476
you will be conducting at Yale.

00:06:46.403 --> 00:06:49.730
However, because we've seen how quickly

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:51.929
our routines and plans can be upended,

00:06:51.929 --> 00:06:54.759
we know that it would be unwise

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:59.176
to think we can completely
map out our paths in life.

00:07:01.116 --> 00:07:05.114
Instead of being weighed
down by uncertainty, though,

00:07:05.114 --> 00:07:08.588
be open to the possibilities ahead.

00:07:08.588 --> 00:07:10.894
View each unexpected challenge

00:07:10.894 --> 00:07:14.138
as a potential to create a change in you

00:07:14.138 --> 00:07:18.055
and in the communities
of which you are a part.

00:07:19.486 --> 00:07:21.356
As you embark on your studies at Yale,

00:07:21.356 --> 00:07:23.110
you will gain knowledge and skills

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.439
that are expected of an
excellent graduate education,

00:07:27.439 --> 00:07:29.524
but you will also have opportunities

00:07:29.524 --> 00:07:31.524
to develop new interests

00:07:32.557 --> 00:07:37.557
and explore unplanned avenues
of inquiry along the way.

00:07:37.692 --> 00:07:39.632
Look for those moments.

00:07:39.632 --> 00:07:41.715
Look for those moments
because you never know

00:07:41.715 --> 00:07:44.632
how significant they will be for you,

00:07:44.632 --> 00:07:49.465
for your local community, for
your country, for the world.

00:07:50.618 --> 00:07:54.789
All of you have come here with
ideas of what you want to do,

00:07:54.789 --> 00:07:58.638
but keep your eyes open
for unexpected paths

00:07:58.638 --> 00:08:00.638
that may unfold for you.

00:08:02.487 --> 00:08:03.679
Since the beginning of the pandemic,

00:08:03.679 --> 00:08:06.740
I've been reminded of the
importance of being receptive

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.431
to unforeseen possibilities.

00:08:09.431 --> 00:08:14.047
Every time I've seen Yale
graduate Francis Collins

00:08:14.047 --> 00:08:18.071
speak about efforts to
control the spread of COVID.

00:08:18.071 --> 00:08:23.071
Many of you know, Dr. Collins
is the acting science advisor

00:08:23.243 --> 00:08:24.588
to President Biden.

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:25.710
He was formerly the director

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.161
of the National Institutes of Health,

00:08:28.161 --> 00:08:29.176
and he's one of the country's

00:08:29.176 --> 00:08:32.215
most respected physician-scientists

00:08:32.215 --> 00:08:35.198
and public health authorities.

00:08:35.198 --> 00:08:37.550
Now, when he was starting
his graduate studies,

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.876
right here at Yale,

00:08:39.876 --> 00:08:42.293
he was in physical chemistry,

00:08:43.863 --> 00:08:47.435
and he said he had no interest
in medicine or biology.

00:08:47.435 --> 00:08:51.204
In fact, he thought biology
was chaotic and unpredictable.

00:08:51.204 --> 00:08:53.829
Sorry, for those of you going into biology

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:56.259
like our provost and our dean.

00:08:56.259 --> 00:08:58.817
(students and faculty chuckle)

00:08:58.817 --> 00:09:00.114
That all changed.

00:09:00.114 --> 00:09:01.742
He took a course in biochemistry.

00:09:01.742 --> 00:09:06.195
It inspired in him a deep
interest in genetics,

00:09:06.195 --> 00:09:09.115
and that led him to
alter his career paths.

00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:13.500
That one biochemistry course
at Yale as a graduate student

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:18.249
became an unexpected
turning point in his life.

00:09:18.249 --> 00:09:20.597
He went on to contribute
to the identification

00:09:20.597 --> 00:09:23.108
of the cystic fibrosis gene,

00:09:23.108 --> 00:09:25.186
that led to the development of a therapy

00:09:25.186 --> 00:09:27.528
that could help tens
of thousands of people

00:09:27.528 --> 00:09:29.810
living with this disease.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:33.970
He was also at the helm of
the Human Genome Project,

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.136
which has given us a detailed knowledge

00:09:36.136 --> 00:09:39.255
about the structure and
function of our genes.

00:09:39.255 --> 00:09:40.159
And in recent years,

00:09:40.159 --> 00:09:43.659
he spearheaded efforts to stop a pandemic.

00:09:46.272 --> 00:09:50.769
What unassuming course
could change your views

00:09:50.769 --> 00:09:54.957
or set you on a new path
in the coming years?

00:09:54.957 --> 00:09:57.624
What could it mean to the world?

00:09:59.093 --> 00:10:02.524
Not all moments of inspiration
will happen inside a library

00:10:02.524 --> 00:10:06.929
or a classroom or a
laboratory or a lecture hall,

00:10:06.929 --> 00:10:10.580
although I hope many will, but
that is why I hope you will

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:14.310
immerse yourself fully
in the Yale community.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:16.004
I think of recent graduates

00:10:16.004 --> 00:10:20.591
who often point to experiences
outside of their departments

00:10:20.591 --> 00:10:24.341
that led them in new
and fruitful directions.

00:10:26.301 --> 00:10:28.675
Lucy Armentado, who received her PhD

00:10:28.675 --> 00:10:31.592
in psychology last year, my department,

00:10:31.592 --> 00:10:33.213
said that serving as the chair

00:10:33.213 --> 00:10:34.850
of the Graduate Student Assembly

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.972
was her most meaningful and
rewarding experience at Yale,

00:10:38.972 --> 00:10:41.484
at least one of the most.

00:10:41.484 --> 00:10:44.426
GSA, she said, was really formative

00:10:44.426 --> 00:10:47.400
in her identity as a graduate student.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.496
She said, "I never
really thought of myself

00:10:49.496 --> 00:10:51.367
as an advocate or leader until GSA.

00:10:51.367 --> 00:10:53.367
And then being GSA chair

00:10:54.324 --> 00:10:57.222
was the most incredible thing
I've done professionally.

00:10:57.222 --> 00:10:58.805
It was a pinnacle."

00:10:59.939 --> 00:11:02.459
Andrew Schartmann came to Yale

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:06.493
with a special interest in music pedagogy.

00:11:06.493 --> 00:11:08.935
He joined the New England
Conservatory's faculty

00:11:08.935 --> 00:11:13.036
after he received his
PhD here in music theory.

00:11:13.036 --> 00:11:17.369
Yet it was a peer in his
graduate class here at Yale

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:23.276
that got him involved in the
Yale School of Medicine project

00:11:23.276 --> 00:11:26.568
designed to promote healthy
lives among young adults

00:11:26.568 --> 00:11:29.189
through virtual reality.

00:11:29.189 --> 00:11:31.655
So in addition to his
teaching of music theory,

00:11:31.655 --> 00:11:34.355
Andrew now serves as the audio director

00:11:34.355 --> 00:11:37.772
of the medical school's Play4REAL XR lab,

00:11:38.851 --> 00:11:43.164
crafting a sound environment
for virtual reality games

00:11:43.164 --> 00:11:45.750
and mobilizing his musical prowess

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.083
in support of public health.

00:11:49.967 --> 00:11:52.215
I think of a student named Edgar Garcia,

00:11:52.215 --> 00:11:53.997
whose academic career, as he said,

00:11:53.997 --> 00:11:57.435
took some unexpected turns along the way.

00:11:57.435 --> 00:12:01.899
He came prepared to study
early American poetics,

00:12:01.899 --> 00:12:04.986
but then a fellowship
at the Beinecke Library

00:12:04.986 --> 00:12:07.868
changed the focus of his PhD work

00:12:07.868 --> 00:12:11.701
to 20th century poetics
and Latinx literature.

00:12:12.633 --> 00:12:15.522
Edgar said the resources of the Beinecke

00:12:15.522 --> 00:12:19.772
made 20th century poetics
more materially resonant,

00:12:20.875 --> 00:12:23.394
and that his English department
mentors and colleagues

00:12:23.394 --> 00:12:28.144
were fully supportive of his
newfound academic interests.

00:12:30.157 --> 00:12:32.621
I think you get the idea.

00:12:32.621 --> 00:12:37.300
You will find that some of
your most memorable moments

00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:40.800
may happen in even more unexpected places.

00:12:42.566 --> 00:12:45.803
I was recently reading an
article about Patrick Njoroge.

00:12:45.803 --> 00:12:49.009
He's the governor of the
central bank of Kenya.

00:12:49.009 --> 00:12:53.509
And he received this PhD in
economics at Yale in 1993.

00:12:55.645 --> 00:12:58.125
And he was talking about
the valuable mentorship

00:12:58.125 --> 00:13:00.051
that he received from faculty members

00:13:00.051 --> 00:13:03.218
like James Tobin and William Brainard.

00:13:04.951 --> 00:13:06.304
But he also was reminiscing

00:13:06.304 --> 00:13:11.264
about attending the Yale-Harvard
football game in 1987.

00:13:11.264 --> 00:13:13.953
"It was cold," he said.

00:13:13.953 --> 00:13:18.953
"It was the coldest day that
I had ever faced in my life.

00:13:19.355 --> 00:13:22.615
It was subzero with a windchill factor.

00:13:22.615 --> 00:13:25.670
I don't even remember who won.

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.003
Survival was the only thing on my mind."

00:13:30.417 --> 00:13:32.205
But survive he did.

00:13:32.205 --> 00:13:33.968
And he used his Yale education

00:13:33.968 --> 00:13:37.164
to help foster economic
opportunity and improve the lives

00:13:37.164 --> 00:13:41.664
of people in Kenya and across
the continent of Africa.

00:13:42.532 --> 00:13:46.433
Be open. Be open to
the options before you.

00:13:46.433 --> 00:13:49.827
Be curious about the work and interests

00:13:49.827 --> 00:13:53.102
of your peers across departments,

00:13:53.102 --> 00:13:56.335
You'll find that Yale fosters
the kind of environment

00:13:56.335 --> 00:14:00.761
that encourages you to pursue
what you came here to do,

00:14:00.761 --> 00:14:03.335
but also offers unexpected ways

00:14:03.335 --> 00:14:06.642
for you to expand your horizons,

00:14:06.642 --> 00:14:07.762
if you immerse yourself

00:14:07.762 --> 00:14:11.059
in the intellectual,
social, and cultural life

00:14:11.059 --> 00:14:13.476
of the university as a whole.

00:14:14.933 --> 00:14:16.871
And don't forget,

00:14:16.871 --> 00:14:20.119
it's also a place you will
make wonderful friends

00:14:20.119 --> 00:14:23.119
and find lifelong mentors.

00:14:23.119 --> 00:14:25.479
I just had lunch two weeks ago

00:14:25.479 --> 00:14:28.005
with my graduate school office mate,

00:14:28.005 --> 00:14:31.305
who sat at a desk next
to me for four years

00:14:31.305 --> 00:14:33.978
in the basement of Kirtland Hall

00:14:33.978 --> 00:14:36.120
in the psychology department.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.787
We've been friends for 40 years.

00:14:41.127 --> 00:14:43.330
I also met Marta, my wife,

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:47.771
when we were both
graduate students at Yale.

00:14:47.771 --> 00:14:51.854
And in 1986, in May, I
submitted my dissertation,

00:14:52.891 --> 00:14:54.891
in June, we got married,

00:14:55.934 --> 00:14:59.517
and in July, I started
on the faculty here.

00:15:01.325 --> 00:15:04.408
So it was a time of great transition,

00:15:05.817 --> 00:15:09.623
but it was the beginning of
the next phase of my wife,

00:15:09.623 --> 00:15:10.685
my life,
(audience chuckles)

00:15:10.685 --> 00:15:12.387
also my wife.

00:15:12.387 --> 00:15:13.874
Marta, why don't you
stand up for a minute,

00:15:13.874 --> 00:15:16.432
and just so people can
see who you are, yeah.

00:15:16.432 --> 00:15:20.349
(students and faculty applaud)

00:15:23.099 --> 00:15:26.794
People think the new dean
of Yale College and I

00:15:26.794 --> 00:15:28.298
are the shortest people on campus,

00:15:28.298 --> 00:15:33.215
but in fact, Marta is really
wearing that with great pride.

00:15:38.697 --> 00:15:42.783
Well, in the coming years, you will learn.

00:15:42.783 --> 00:15:44.501
You will grow.

00:15:44.501 --> 00:15:47.631
You will find surprising
opportunities and challenges.

00:15:47.631 --> 00:15:49.048
I guarantee that.

00:15:50.275 --> 00:15:54.133
But what I can't predict is
what you will do ultimately

00:15:54.133 --> 00:15:56.857
with your Yale graduate education,

00:15:56.857 --> 00:15:59.790
with the knowledge, with the
expertise, with the experience,

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:02.290
with the wisdom you will gain.

00:16:05.142 --> 00:16:07.584
What we do know is that Yale graduates

00:16:07.584 --> 00:16:11.344
have gone on to lead in business,
in science, in technology,

00:16:11.344 --> 00:16:14.261
in the arts, and in the humanities.

00:16:15.495 --> 00:16:17.828
They are leaving their mark.

00:16:18.739 --> 00:16:23.166
They are realizing
Yale's vision and mission

00:16:23.166 --> 00:16:26.863
to educate aspiring leaders worldwide,

00:16:26.863 --> 00:16:29.613
who serve all sectors of society.

00:16:33.157 --> 00:16:35.017
It's gonna go quickly.

00:16:35.017 --> 00:16:39.166
Before you know it, you will be graduating

00:16:39.166 --> 00:16:42.499
and you will take your ideas and dreams,

00:16:43.365 --> 00:16:45.363
incubated at Yale.

00:16:45.363 --> 00:16:50.030
You will take them and bring
them to life in novel ways.

00:16:54.457 --> 00:16:58.844
I'm counting on you to
improve the world today

00:16:58.844 --> 00:17:01.094
and for future generations.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:07.270
I can't wait to see what you
are all going to accomplish.

00:17:09.904 --> 00:17:14.293
And on behalf of all of my
colleagues here on stage with me,

00:17:14.293 --> 00:17:16.210
we welcome you to Yale.

00:17:17.345 --> 00:17:21.262
(students and faculty applaud)

00:17:26.645 --> 00:17:27.833
Thank you.

00:17:27.833 --> 00:17:32.352
And now it is a great pleasure
for me to welcome you,

00:17:32.352 --> 00:17:37.070
once again, by introducing
you to your dean, Lynn Cooley.

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.434
Your dean, now in her second term

00:17:39.434 --> 00:17:44.434
as the Dean of the Graduate
School of Arts and Sciences,

00:17:44.667 --> 00:17:47.513
she's a tireless advocate
for graduate students,

00:17:47.513 --> 00:17:51.133
a powerful agent of change
in graduate education

00:17:51.133 --> 00:17:52.216
here at Yale.

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:56.397
Dean Cooley is the C.N.H.
Long Professor of Genetics.

00:17:56.397 --> 00:17:59.135
She's also a Professor of Cell Biology

00:17:59.135 --> 00:18:03.305
and of Molecular, Cellular,
and Developmental Biology.

00:18:03.305 --> 00:18:06.976
And she's the Vice Provost
for Postdoctoral Affairs.

00:18:06.976 --> 00:18:08.416
She was, for many years,

00:18:08.416 --> 00:18:10.778
the director of Yale's Combined Program

00:18:10.778 --> 00:18:14.195
in the Biological and Biomedical Sciences

00:18:15.294 --> 00:18:18.719
Dean Cooley earned her BA
from Connecticut College,

00:18:18.719 --> 00:18:21.310
her PhD from the University of Texas,

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:23.217
and she carried out research

00:18:23.217 --> 00:18:25.346
with Sterling Professor Dieter Soll,

00:18:25.346 --> 00:18:29.001
here in Yale's Department
of Molecular Biophysics

00:18:29.001 --> 00:18:30.418
and Biochemistry.

00:18:31.277 --> 00:18:32.899
After post-doctoral training

00:18:32.899 --> 00:18:35.681
at the Carnegie Institution of Science,

00:18:35.681 --> 00:18:37.511
she joined the Yale faculty

00:18:37.511 --> 00:18:39.361
to study cellular mechanisms

00:18:39.361 --> 00:18:42.444
underlying female and male fertility.

00:18:43.303 --> 00:18:47.414
She's been recognized
with a Pew Scholar Award

00:18:47.414 --> 00:18:49.000
from the Pew Charitable Trust.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:51.327
She's a member of the Connecticut Academy

00:18:51.327 --> 00:18:52.857
for Science and Engineering

00:18:52.857 --> 00:18:55.415
and a fellow of the American Association

00:18:55.415 --> 00:18:57.748
for the Advancement of Science.

00:18:57.748 --> 00:18:58.826
She served on the board

00:18:58.826 --> 00:19:01.688
of the Genetics Society
of America for many years,

00:19:01.688 --> 00:19:04.521
and she was the president in 2017.

00:19:07.153 --> 00:19:10.010
In the spirit of my speech just now,

00:19:10.010 --> 00:19:13.056
Dean Cooley has many other interests.

00:19:13.056 --> 00:19:16.810
She's an avid member of the
Appalachian Mountain Club.

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.675
She's a student of modern dance.

00:19:19.675 --> 00:19:21.687
She's a devoted mother of two daughters,

00:19:21.687 --> 00:19:24.020
who are both Yale graduates.

00:19:24.991 --> 00:19:27.137
And it gives me great pleasure

00:19:27.137 --> 00:19:29.941
to introduce you to your dean

00:19:29.941 --> 00:19:32.960
and one of Yale's
outstanding faculty members,

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:34.111
Lynn Cooley.

00:19:34.111 --> 00:19:38.028
(students and faculty applaud)

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:42.473
- Thank you very much.

00:19:46.850 --> 00:19:48.628
Thank you, President Salovey,

00:19:48.628 --> 00:19:52.966
for your remarks and that
wonderful introduction.

00:19:52.966 --> 00:19:55.547
Good morning, distinguished colleagues,

00:19:55.547 --> 00:19:57.952
and good morning to you,

00:19:57.952 --> 00:20:01.235
new master's degree
students, doctoral students,

00:20:01.235 --> 00:20:02.566
and visiting students

00:20:02.566 --> 00:20:05.520
in the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.954
We are thrilled to welcome you

00:20:07.954 --> 00:20:11.279
as the newest members of our community.

00:20:11.279 --> 00:20:14.058
You should be proud of all your hard work

00:20:14.058 --> 00:20:16.391
that brought you here today.

00:20:17.526 --> 00:20:20.669
With deep gratitude and respect,

00:20:20.669 --> 00:20:22.638
I want to begin by acknowledging

00:20:22.638 --> 00:20:25.531
the indigenous peoples and nations,

00:20:25.531 --> 00:20:30.531
including Mohegan, Mashantucket
Pequot, Eastern Pequot,

00:20:30.824 --> 00:20:34.844
Schaghticoke, Golden
Hill Paugussett, Niantic,

00:20:34.844 --> 00:20:38.586
and the Quinnipiac and other
Algonquian speaking peoples,

00:20:38.586 --> 00:20:41.370
who have stewarded through generations

00:20:41.370 --> 00:20:44.156
the lands and waterways of what is now

00:20:44.156 --> 00:20:47.808
the state of Connecticut
and Yale University.

00:20:47.808 --> 00:20:50.861
We honor the enduring
relationship that exists

00:20:50.861 --> 00:20:55.048
between these peoples
and nations and this land

00:20:55.048 --> 00:20:57.940
and recognize that this relationship

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:00.503
is as much a part of our history

00:21:00.503 --> 00:21:04.003
as it is a part of our present and future.

00:21:05.362 --> 00:21:07.360
Graduate students,

00:21:07.360 --> 00:21:10.449
you were selected to be here

00:21:10.449 --> 00:21:15.449
because you have proven
abilities and unlimited promise.

00:21:15.776 --> 00:21:18.551
You are here because our faculty

00:21:18.551 --> 00:21:23.551
recognized your intellectual
curiosity and creativity,

00:21:23.832 --> 00:21:26.332
your academic drive and rigor,

00:21:27.172 --> 00:21:29.033
your passion for discovery,

00:21:29.033 --> 00:21:32.236
and your potential for leadership.

00:21:32.236 --> 00:21:36.487
You are here because
we believe you can make

00:21:36.487 --> 00:21:41.046
valuable contributions to
both Yale and the world,

00:21:41.046 --> 00:21:44.296
and because we know Yale can help you

00:21:44.296 --> 00:21:47.234
on your path to intellectual growth.

00:21:47.234 --> 00:21:50.817
We're delighted you
have chosen to join us.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:53.913
The graduate school is at the very heart

00:21:53.913 --> 00:21:56.871
of Yale's mission as a university.

00:21:56.871 --> 00:22:00.012
Our mission is to
educate graduate students

00:22:00.012 --> 00:22:04.895
to seek answers to life's
most challenging questions.

00:22:04.895 --> 00:22:06.798
As you join us here today,

00:22:06.798 --> 00:22:09.469
you are setting off on
a chapter of your life

00:22:09.469 --> 00:22:13.483
that will allow you to
explore a field in depth,

00:22:13.483 --> 00:22:16.131
master an area of inquiry,

00:22:16.131 --> 00:22:18.011
and learn to disseminate knowledge

00:22:18.011 --> 00:22:20.678
within and beyond the classroom.

00:22:21.535 --> 00:22:24.311
This year's incoming class
in the graduate school

00:22:24.311 --> 00:22:26.394
has 535 doctoral students

00:22:27.948 --> 00:22:30.115
and 219 master's students.

00:22:31.236 --> 00:22:34.569
Collectively, you represent 54 countries

00:22:35.986 --> 00:22:39.012
and 39 states of the United States,

00:22:39.012 --> 00:22:42.262
plus Washington, D.C., and Puerto Rico.

00:22:43.501 --> 00:22:47.724
54% of you are international students.

00:22:47.724 --> 00:22:50.301
You will be working across
the graduate school's

00:22:50.301 --> 00:22:53.767
more than 70 departments and programs.

00:22:53.767 --> 00:22:57.198
But whatever your area of study may be,

00:22:57.198 --> 00:22:59.261
proteins or poetry,

00:22:59.261 --> 00:23:02.231
ecosystems or solar systems,

00:23:02.231 --> 00:23:05.257
you all share a common motivation.

00:23:05.257 --> 00:23:08.631
You're driven by the desire to learn more,

00:23:08.631 --> 00:23:12.610
by the thrill of discovering
new truths about our world,

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:16.261
and by the desire to
stretch your imagination

00:23:16.261 --> 00:23:18.344
and create new knowledge.

00:23:20.516 --> 00:23:23.600
So reflecting on the
composition of this class,

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:24.591
I can tell you,

00:23:24.591 --> 00:23:28.775
Yale is fortunate to
gather the best students

00:23:28.775 --> 00:23:31.147
from across the globe.

00:23:31.147 --> 00:23:34.222
Which means, if you look around you,

00:23:34.222 --> 00:23:37.036
you come from all walks of life,

00:23:37.036 --> 00:23:39.369
backgrounds, and identities.

00:23:40.443 --> 00:23:43.699
The graduate school is committed
to creating an environment

00:23:43.699 --> 00:23:47.116
in which all of you can thrive and excel,

00:23:49.007 --> 00:23:52.750
Partly because we value
diversity, equity, inclusion,

00:23:52.750 --> 00:23:56.859
and belonging as a core
principle of the university,

00:23:56.859 --> 00:23:59.743
but also because research shows

00:23:59.743 --> 00:24:02.565
that groups who embody these qualities

00:24:02.565 --> 00:24:06.398
are better investigators
and smarter thinkers.

00:24:08.046 --> 00:24:10.471
I had my own eye-opening experience

00:24:10.471 --> 00:24:13.301
as a graduate student many years ago,

00:24:13.301 --> 00:24:15.735
when I found myself in a laboratory

00:24:15.735 --> 00:24:19.492
where I was one of a small
minority of Americans,

00:24:19.492 --> 00:24:23.524
surrounded by people
from all over the world.

00:24:23.524 --> 00:24:28.524
I learned things like a few
phrases in German and Japanese,

00:24:29.243 --> 00:24:33.098
but I also learned that
each of our backgrounds

00:24:33.098 --> 00:24:36.582
and experiences meant
we could each contribute

00:24:36.582 --> 00:24:40.894
in our own ways to the culture of the lab.

00:24:40.894 --> 00:24:44.060
My lab mates got a kick out
of learning some phrases

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.229
in American Sign Language from me.

00:24:47.229 --> 00:24:52.229
I still remember when one of
the postdocs I greatly admired

00:24:52.633 --> 00:24:56.633
realized his experiment
had crashed for the day.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:00.038
And instead of being forlorn,

00:25:00.038 --> 00:25:02.433
he grinned at me across the lab

00:25:02.433 --> 00:25:05.183
and signed, "Free day," and left.

00:25:08.922 --> 00:25:10.271
His reaction showed me

00:25:10.271 --> 00:25:12.593
that the give-and-take
of different languages

00:25:12.593 --> 00:25:15.047
helped create an atmosphere of trust

00:25:15.047 --> 00:25:17.028
in which we could comfortably share

00:25:17.028 --> 00:25:19.941
research ideas and anything else,

00:25:19.941 --> 00:25:23.195
no matter how crazy they seemed.

00:25:23.195 --> 00:25:27.943
It also showed me it was
okay to take some free time

00:25:27.943 --> 00:25:30.749
when you hit a temporary roadblock.

00:25:30.749 --> 00:25:33.530
Both were valuable lessons to me

00:25:33.530 --> 00:25:36.780
as a young person in a new environment.

00:25:38.341 --> 00:25:39.789
It can be overwhelming

00:25:39.789 --> 00:25:42.070
to start this new chapter in your life.

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:44.596
You're in a new city, for most of you,

00:25:44.596 --> 00:25:46.503
meeting lots of new people,

00:25:46.503 --> 00:25:48.283
and figuring out how to navigate

00:25:48.283 --> 00:25:51.340
the first steps of graduate school.

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.590
Trust that you belong here.

00:25:54.831 --> 00:25:57.723
Trust that bringing your whole self

00:25:57.723 --> 00:26:00.056
will make everything better.

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:04.698
Trust that you will contribute positively

00:26:04.698 --> 00:26:06.190
to the people around you,

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:10.029
based on your experiences
and perspectives.

00:26:10.029 --> 00:26:11.654
Learn to listen.

00:26:11.654 --> 00:26:14.310
Learn when to listen and when to speak up,

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:15.934
to put yourself out there

00:26:15.934 --> 00:26:19.935
and start conversations with
people you don't know yet.

00:26:19.935 --> 00:26:21.940
Over the next few days,

00:26:21.940 --> 00:26:25.377
be intentional about meeting new people

00:26:25.377 --> 00:26:29.116
and be open to building
relationships with them.

00:26:29.116 --> 00:26:30.471
Some of those relationships

00:26:30.471 --> 00:26:33.443
may lead to future collaborations

00:26:33.443 --> 00:26:38.110
and other may lead to lifelong
friends or even marriage.

00:26:40.829 --> 00:26:42.692
The human connections you form,

00:26:42.692 --> 00:26:45.196
be they personal or professional,

00:26:45.196 --> 00:26:46.613
will sustain you.

00:26:48.179 --> 00:26:50.559
These human connections can also help you

00:26:50.559 --> 00:26:55.559
when discussing fraught or
complex issues with your peers.

00:26:55.631 --> 00:26:59.243
Yale is committed to the
free exchange of ideas,

00:26:59.243 --> 00:27:03.235
and it is the foundation upon
which the university exists

00:27:03.235 --> 00:27:06.394
in its educational enterprise.

00:27:06.394 --> 00:27:10.591
Yale's official statement
on freedom of expression,

00:27:10.591 --> 00:27:13.612
commonly referred to
as the Woodward Report,

00:27:13.612 --> 00:27:17.811
makes the following
statement that I quote,

00:27:17.811 --> 00:27:21.974
"By voluntarily taking up
membership in a university

00:27:21.974 --> 00:27:26.556
and thereby asserting a claim
to its rights and privileges,

00:27:26.556 --> 00:27:29.235
members also acknowledge the existence

00:27:29.235 --> 00:27:34.214
of certain obligations upon
themselves and their fellows.

00:27:34.214 --> 00:27:39.214
Above all, every member of the
university has an obligation

00:27:39.382 --> 00:27:43.028
to permit free expression
in the university.

00:27:43.028 --> 00:27:47.923
No member has a right to
prevent such expression,"

00:27:47.923 --> 00:27:48.923
close quote.

00:27:49.977 --> 00:27:54.350
Questioning your assumptions
about what you currently know

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:57.129
will broaden your
understanding of the world,

00:27:57.129 --> 00:27:59.977
both in the classroom
and in your interactions

00:27:59.977 --> 00:28:03.503
with those you encounter
in your daily lives.

00:28:03.503 --> 00:28:05.464
You have made it to graduate school

00:28:05.464 --> 00:28:08.728
because you excelled intellectually,

00:28:08.728 --> 00:28:09.882
but Yale is a place

00:28:09.882 --> 00:28:14.802
that requires you to be
emotionally intelligent as well.

00:28:14.802 --> 00:28:18.585
You may hear passionate
opinions about campus issues

00:28:18.585 --> 00:28:20.867
or issues playing out across the country

00:28:20.867 --> 00:28:22.742
and around the world.

00:28:22.742 --> 00:28:25.549
Be willing to listen to a range of views

00:28:25.549 --> 00:28:27.870
and develop your own opinions,

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:32.141
even if it means agreeing to
disagree from time to time.

00:28:32.141 --> 00:28:34.956
Disagreement does not inhibit growth

00:28:34.956 --> 00:28:37.230
or disregard mutual respect.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:41.230
We cannot lose sight of
our collective humanity.

00:28:43.591 --> 00:28:47.956
If you feel a bit unsure
about what is to come,

00:28:47.956 --> 00:28:50.811
this is absolutely normal.

00:28:50.811 --> 00:28:55.181
In fact, that feeling is
the perfect starting point

00:28:55.181 --> 00:28:57.938
for what you have come here to do.

00:28:57.938 --> 00:28:59.907
Your time in graduate school

00:28:59.907 --> 00:29:02.410
is very much about getting comfortable

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:07.160
with uncertainty and dwelling
at the edge of the unknown.

00:29:08.971 --> 00:29:12.801
Don't think we have a
single word for that state,

00:29:12.801 --> 00:29:17.134
but we can describe it as
a tolerance for ambiguity.

00:29:19.802 --> 00:29:22.466
The romantic poet, John Keats,

00:29:22.466 --> 00:29:24.966
called it negative capability,

00:29:25.985 --> 00:29:27.935
or the capacity to thrive

00:29:27.935 --> 00:29:31.483
in the midst of uncertainty and doubt.

00:29:31.483 --> 00:29:33.866
It may sound like an unpleasant state,

00:29:33.866 --> 00:29:36.683
but Keats and others after him

00:29:36.683 --> 00:29:41.683
understood negative capability
as a positive prerequisite

00:29:42.067 --> 00:29:44.484
for discovery and creativity.

00:29:45.529 --> 00:29:47.960
One of our aims in the graduate school

00:29:47.960 --> 00:29:49.796
is to help you develop

00:29:49.796 --> 00:29:53.838
the negative capability
you need to be comfortable

00:29:53.838 --> 00:29:57.379
when faced with uncertainty and mystery,

00:29:57.379 --> 00:30:01.780
to become the scholars and
researchers you aspire to be,

00:30:01.780 --> 00:30:04.305
who can push forward the boundaries

00:30:04.305 --> 00:30:08.988
of what we know and
understand about the world.

00:30:08.988 --> 00:30:12.607
That search can truly be
disconcerting and difficult,

00:30:12.607 --> 00:30:16.444
but don't insist on certainty
where there is none.

00:30:16.444 --> 00:30:19.817
Nothing great comes out of
being entirely comfortable

00:30:19.817 --> 00:30:21.303
all the time.

00:30:21.303 --> 00:30:24.470
Some days will just not go as planned.

00:30:25.545 --> 00:30:28.024
Theories and experiments may not take you

00:30:28.024 --> 00:30:30.671
where you expect to go.

00:30:30.671 --> 00:30:34.093
I have learned this
lesson over and over again

00:30:34.093 --> 00:30:37.711
in my time as a research scientist.

00:30:37.711 --> 00:30:41.399
In fact, the most exciting days in my lab

00:30:41.399 --> 00:30:46.066
are when an unexpected result
leads to a brand new area.

00:30:47.128 --> 00:30:51.447
Most recently, investigating
the function of a gene

00:30:51.447 --> 00:30:55.864
required for egg development
and fertility in females

00:30:57.308 --> 00:31:02.032
has led us to studying
brain-specific protein synthesis

00:31:02.032 --> 00:31:04.235
in a way that wasn't possible before.

00:31:04.235 --> 00:31:08.544
I never would've imagined that transition.

00:31:08.544 --> 00:31:10.078
The network you create

00:31:10.078 --> 00:31:12.813
and the departmental home you have joined

00:31:12.813 --> 00:31:14.897
will give you the support you need

00:31:14.897 --> 00:31:18.711
to survive and thrive in graduate school.

00:31:18.711 --> 00:31:21.173
You will find that graduate
school is very different

00:31:21.173 --> 00:31:24.438
from your previous experiences.

00:31:24.438 --> 00:31:28.105
You will be much more responsible
for your own education

00:31:28.105 --> 00:31:30.124
than you ever have been before.

00:31:30.124 --> 00:31:32.166
Your schedules will be less predictable

00:31:32.166 --> 00:31:34.374
than they were in the past.

00:31:34.374 --> 00:31:39.374
This is not an easy endeavor,
and it isn't meant to be one.

00:31:39.772 --> 00:31:42.511
There will be challenges
and obstacles ahead,

00:31:42.511 --> 00:31:45.806
but trust that you will move forward.

00:31:45.806 --> 00:31:48.507
If you start to think you're in it alone,

00:31:48.507 --> 00:31:52.924
remember you are surrounded
by mentors and advocates,

00:31:53.909 --> 00:31:56.044
in the form of faculty members,

00:31:56.044 --> 00:31:59.195
many of whom are here with me today,

00:31:59.195 --> 00:32:03.957
staff and peers, just
look around the lawn here.

00:32:03.957 --> 00:32:06.697
The peers that you will find here,

00:32:06.697 --> 00:32:09.618
along with faculty and staff,

00:32:09.618 --> 00:32:14.618
are here to support you and
offer advice when you need it.

00:32:14.767 --> 00:32:17.672
Look to those around you
for inspiration and guidance

00:32:17.672 --> 00:32:19.985
and you will find it.

00:32:19.985 --> 00:32:24.282
You're are about to embark
on a marathon, not a sprint.

00:32:24.282 --> 00:32:25.637
Pace yourself.

00:32:25.637 --> 00:32:27.826
Be kind to yourself and others,

00:32:27.826 --> 00:32:30.659
and ask for help when you need it.

00:32:33.098 --> 00:32:34.891
Though obstacles will exist,

00:32:34.891 --> 00:32:38.000
great solutions to the world's
most challenging problems

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:40.752
will emerge from your time here.

00:32:40.752 --> 00:32:43.296
As graduate students,
you hold a special place

00:32:43.296 --> 00:32:44.794
in the university landscape

00:32:44.794 --> 00:32:48.847
at the intersection of all
the research and teaching

00:32:48.847 --> 00:32:50.976
happening across campus.

00:32:50.976 --> 00:32:54.023
You're not simply here to
learn from the faculty.

00:32:54.023 --> 00:32:56.863
You're here to learn with them.

00:32:56.863 --> 00:33:01.299
Your fresh insights and
persistent curiosity

00:33:01.299 --> 00:33:02.646
are the catalysts

00:33:02.646 --> 00:33:07.063
to new ways of thinking,
learning, and creating.

00:33:07.063 --> 00:33:09.639
We are eager to witness and celebrate

00:33:09.639 --> 00:33:12.056
all that you will accomplish.

00:33:13.272 --> 00:33:14.952
And the world will be waiting

00:33:14.952 --> 00:33:17.438
for you to come out and
share what you've learned,

00:33:17.438 --> 00:33:19.341
as soon as you're ready.

00:33:19.341 --> 00:33:21.550
The empathy you develop
from being immersed

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:25.445
in a diverse community
will better position you

00:33:25.445 --> 00:33:29.781
to offer solutions in ways
that promote peace and dignity

00:33:29.781 --> 00:33:31.697
for all concerned.

00:33:31.697 --> 00:33:33.901
You will become better communicators

00:33:33.901 --> 00:33:35.659
in order to share your findings

00:33:35.659 --> 00:33:37.461
with colleagues in your own field,

00:33:37.461 --> 00:33:41.797
but very importantly,
the lay public as well.

00:33:41.797 --> 00:33:45.586
Recent history has shown us
that we all must devote energy

00:33:45.586 --> 00:33:48.669
toward helping leaders make decisions

00:33:49.608 --> 00:33:51.775
based on fact and insight.

00:33:54.606 --> 00:33:56.374
I want to echo President Salovey

00:33:56.374 --> 00:34:00.450
by encouraging you to participate
fully in our community

00:34:00.450 --> 00:34:04.941
in ways that suit your personal
and academic goals best.

00:34:04.941 --> 00:34:07.471
During your orientation week,

00:34:07.471 --> 00:34:10.920
you'll learn there are many
student clubs, institutes,

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:13.315
and organizations to explore.

00:34:13.315 --> 00:34:15.511
You will undoubtedly be able to find

00:34:15.511 --> 00:34:18.594
one or more that match your interest.

00:34:19.792 --> 00:34:22.539
If you're interested in student advocacy,

00:34:22.539 --> 00:34:24.144
truly improving the lives

00:34:24.144 --> 00:34:27.119
of your fellow graduate
students during your time,

00:34:27.119 --> 00:34:29.393
the Graduate Student Assembly, or GSA,

00:34:29.393 --> 00:34:33.976
works closely with my office
to identify student priorities

00:34:33.976 --> 00:34:36.545
and address them as quickly as possible.

00:34:36.545 --> 00:34:39.936
Lucy Armentado, who
President Salovey mentioned,

00:34:39.936 --> 00:34:44.936
was an incredible leader during
her time leading the GSA.

00:34:45.328 --> 00:34:46.571
Throughout the pandemic,

00:34:46.571 --> 00:34:49.618
my office collaborated
with GSA representatives

00:34:49.618 --> 00:34:53.489
to respond to student needs,
such as increased stipends,

00:34:53.489 --> 00:34:55.475
enhanced childcare support,

00:34:55.475 --> 00:34:58.248
and improved mental health resources.

00:34:58.248 --> 00:35:00.364
We accomplished these things together

00:35:00.364 --> 00:35:04.688
through trust, collaboration, and honesty.

00:35:04.688 --> 00:35:06.126
Student government works here,

00:35:06.126 --> 00:35:08.626
and I urge you to participate.

00:35:10.203 --> 00:35:12.525
As you become immersed in your research

00:35:12.525 --> 00:35:13.747
and in our community,

00:35:13.747 --> 00:35:14.811
I also encourage you

00:35:14.811 --> 00:35:18.644
to keep your eye on
your future after Yale .

00:35:18.644 --> 00:35:21.784
Though this is your first
official day of graduate school,

00:35:21.784 --> 00:35:25.744
it is not too early to start
investigating and planning

00:35:25.744 --> 00:35:27.520
your next chapter.

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:31.325
Visit our Office of Career
Strategy for programs and advice

00:35:31.325 --> 00:35:35.132
about careers within
and beyond the academy.

00:35:35.132 --> 00:35:38.584
They provide invaluable guidance,
professional development,

00:35:38.584 --> 00:35:43.417
and concrete plans of action
for anyone building a career.

00:35:44.559 --> 00:35:47.148
The graduate school also
has highly accomplished

00:35:47.148 --> 00:35:51.915
and dedicated alumni who come
back to Yale to help you.

00:35:51.915 --> 00:35:53.488
Later this year,

00:35:53.488 --> 00:35:56.709
you will be hearing about
the Alumni Association's

00:35:56.709 --> 00:35:59.469
"Where Do I Go from Yale" workshop

00:35:59.469 --> 00:36:02.339
held each spring semester.

00:36:02.339 --> 00:36:04.168
This will give you a chance to see

00:36:04.168 --> 00:36:06.805
what our amazing alumni are doing.

00:36:06.805 --> 00:36:11.805
People who were once sitting
right where you are today.

00:36:12.178 --> 00:36:13.769
They can inspire your work

00:36:13.769 --> 00:36:16.554
and give you ideas for the future.

00:36:16.554 --> 00:36:20.154
Until then, tap into
our vast alumni network

00:36:20.154 --> 00:36:22.274
through the Cross Campus platform.

00:36:22.274 --> 00:36:24.011
It's a great way to make connections

00:36:24.011 --> 00:36:26.399
with alumni in your fields of interest

00:36:26.399 --> 00:36:29.463
who are willing to offer advice, ideas,

00:36:29.463 --> 00:36:33.256
and all the wisdom of
someone who has been there.

00:36:33.256 --> 00:36:37.358
They are great people and
they can open your eyes

00:36:37.358 --> 00:36:41.441
to opportunities you may
not have considered yet.

00:36:43.082 --> 00:36:47.135
To close, today is the
start of an exciting journey

00:36:47.135 --> 00:36:49.791
for all of you graduate students.

00:36:49.791 --> 00:36:54.224
Seize the opportunities to
grow, to challenge yourself,

00:36:54.224 --> 00:36:58.062
and be sure to celebrate
not only your own successes,

00:36:58.062 --> 00:37:01.404
but those of your colleagues and friends.

00:37:01.404 --> 00:37:03.208
Yale will have an impact on you,

00:37:03.208 --> 00:37:06.306
but you will also have
an impact on this campus.

00:37:06.306 --> 00:37:10.766
And after you graduate,
on all sectors of society.

00:37:10.766 --> 00:37:12.776
We welcome you to this community

00:37:12.776 --> 00:37:17.026
and we look forward to all
that's in store for you.

00:37:18.358 --> 00:37:22.275
(students and faculty applaud)

